<SiteLayout title="Module 3: Protocols and Models — fred.fish" description="A source-traceable lesson on protocol rules, OSI and TCP/IP models, encapsulation, and network addressing." active="cisco">
  <section class="source-lesson">
    <div class="lesson-nav"><a href="/cisco/learning/semester-1/">← Semester 1 course</a><a href="/cisco/practice/">Adaptive practice ↗</a></div>
    <header class="lesson-hero"><Eyebrow index="S1 / 03" label="MODULE 3 / PROTOCOLS AND MODELS" /><h1>Understand the rules.<br /><span>Follow the data.</span></h1><p>Protocols define how devices communicate. Models help explain how those protocols cooperate as data moves from an application, through a network, and to another host.</p></header>
    <section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">01</div><div><h2>Protocols define communication rules</h2><p>A protocol describes the rules used by communicating devices. Communication requires a sender and receiver, and protocols define message encoding, formatting and encapsulation, message size, timing, and delivery options. Multiple protocols cooperate to deliver one application message. A protocol suite is a group of related protocols; TCP/IP is the protocol suite used by modern networks. Open standards support interoperability between equipment and software from different vendors.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGE</span><code>IMG-0218 · IMG-0254</code></div></div></section>
    <section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">02</div><div><h2>Compare OSI and TCP/IP reference models</h2><p>The OSI model has seven layers: Application, Presentation, Session, Transport, Network, Data Link, and Physical. The TCP/IP model groups networking functions into four layers: Application, Transport, Internet, and Network Access. These are conceptual models, not physical devices. They provide a shared vocabulary for understanding where a protocol operates and how responsibilities are separated.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGE</span><code>IMG-0248 · IMG-0269</code></div></div></section>
    <section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">03</div><div><h2>Understand encapsulation and PDUs</h2><p>As data moves down the protocol stack, each layer adds the control information it needs. Application data is passed to the transport layer, which adds a TCP or UDP header to form a segment. The network layer adds an IP header to form a packet. The data link layer adds a frame header and trailer, including MAC addressing. The physical layer transmits bits as signals. The common PDU names are data, segment, packet, frame, and bits. At the receiver, decapsulation removes these headers in reverse order.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGE</span><code>IMG-0256 · IMG-0270 · IMG-0271</code></div></div></section>
    <section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">04</div><div><h2>Separate Layer 2 and Layer 3 addressing</h2><p>IP addressing identifies the source and ultimate destination across networks. MAC addressing delivers a frame across the local link. When the destination is on the same IP network, the sender can address the frame to the destination host's MAC address. When the destination is remote, the sender keeps the remote host's IP address in the packet but sends the local Ethernet frame to the default gateway's MAC address. Routers remove the incoming link-layer frame and build a new frame for the next link.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGE</span><code>IMG-0195 · IMG-0201 · IMG-0202</code></div></div></section>
    <section class="model-compare"><div><Eyebrow label="OSI MODEL" /><h2>7 layers</h2><ol><li>Application</li><li>Presentation</li><li>Session</li><li>Transport</li><li>Network</li><li>Data Link</li><li>Physical</li></ol></div><div><Eyebrow label="TCP/IP MODEL" /><h2>4 layers</h2><ol><li>Application</li><li>Transport</li><li>Internet</li><li>Network Access</li></ol></div></section>
    <section class="lesson-end"><div><Eyebrow label="CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING" /><h2>Trace a message through the stack.</h2><p>Practice the layer responsibilities, PDU names, and the difference between IP and MAC addressing.</p></div><a href="/cisco/practice/">Start practice →</a></section>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
